(>> Solution to Question 1:) Tj
T*
ET
BT
/F1 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(   [Problem Statement]: Differentiate between prokaryotic and eukaryotic cellular architecture regarding compartmentalization and genetic material organization.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   Prokaryotic and eukaryotic cells represent the two primary phylogenetic architectures of life:) Tj
T*
(   1. Compartmentalization: Eukaryotes possess extensive membrane-bound internal compartments) Tj
T*
(   \(organelles\) such as the nucleus, endoplasmic reticulum \(ER\), Golgi apparatus, lysosomes,) Tj
T*
(   mitochondria, and peroxisomes. These separate specialized biochemical reactions \(e.g., acid) Tj
T*
(   hydrolase activity in lysosomes vs ATP synthesis in mitochondria\). Prokaryotes lack) Tj
T*
(   membrane-enclosed organelles; their biochemical pathways occur in the cytoplasm or along) Tj
T*
(   invaginations of the plasma membrane \(mesosomes\).) Tj
T*
(   2. Organization of Genetic Material: In eukaryotes, genomic DNA is linear, organized around) Tj
T*
(   octameric histone cores into nucleosomal chromatin, and enclosed within a double-membrane) Tj
T*
(   nuclear envelope with nuclear pore complexes. In prokaryotes, genetic material consists of a) Tj
T*
(   single circular, double-stranded DNA chromosome localized in an irregularly shaped cytoplasmic) Tj
T*
(   region called the nucleoid, devoid of a nuclear membrane and true histones \(utilizing) Tj
T*
(   histone-like HU proteins instead\).) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(--------------------------------------------------------------------------------) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
ET
BT
/F2 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(>> Solution to Question 2:) Tj
T*
ET
BT
/F1 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(   [Problem Statement]: Describe the Fluid Mosaic Model of Singer and Nicolson \(1972\) and explain the biochemical factors influencing membrane fluidity.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   The Fluid Mosaic Model conceptualizes biological membranes as two-dimensional liquid solutions) Tj
T*
(   of oriented lipids and globular proteins:) Tj
T*
(   1. Structural Organization: The amphipathic phospholipid bilayer forms a continuous fluid) Tj
T*
(   matrix with hydrophobic hydrocarbon tails facing inward and polar head groups interacting with) Tj
T*
(   aqueous phases. Integral membrane proteins are embedded within or span the bilayer, while) Tj
T*
(   peripheral proteins associate via electrostatic and hydrogen-bonding interactions with lipid) Tj
T*
(   heads or integral proteins.) Tj
T*
(   2. Factors Influencing Membrane Fluidity:) Tj
T*
(   - Fatty Acid Saturation: Cis-unsaturated double bonds introduce 30-degree kinks in acyl chains,) Tj
T*
(   preventing tight crystal packing and lowering the phase transition temperature \(Tm\), thereby) Tj
T*
(   increasing fluidity. Saturated acyl chains pack tightly via van der Waals forces, increasing) Tj
T*
(   rigidity.) Tj
T*
(   - Acyl Chain Length: Longer hydrocarbon chains increase hydrophobic interaction surface area,) Tj
T*
(   elevating Tm and decreasing fluidity.) Tj
T*
(   - Cholesterol Content: Cholesterol acts as a bidirectional fluidity buffer. Below Tm, its bulky) Tj
T*
(   steroid ring prevents close acyl packing, inhibiting gel crystallization. Above Tm, its rigid) Tj
T*
(   planar ring restricts excessive acyl motion, reducing membrane permeability and fluidity.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(--------------------------------------------------------------------------------) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
ET
BT
/F2 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(>> Solution to Question 3:) Tj
T*
ET
BT
/F1 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(   [Problem Statement]: Derive the thermodynamic equation for uncharged solute transport across a biological membrane and calculate Delta G for an inward 10-fold concentration gradient.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   Thermodynamic Formulation for Uncharged Solutes:) Tj
T*
(   1. Chemical Potential: The chemical potential mu of a solute at concentration [C] is given by) Tj
T*
(   mu = mu_0 + R*T*ln[C].) Tj
T*
(   2. Free Energy of Transport: For translocation of 1 mole of uncharged solute from the) Tj
T*
(   extracellular medium \(out\) to cytoplasm \(in\):) Tj
T*
(   Delta G = mu_in - mu_out = R*T*ln\([C]in / [C]out\).) Tj
T*
ET

endstream
