(   [Problem Statement]: Contrast simple diffusion, facilitated diffusion, primary active transport, and secondary active transport with biological examples.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   Comparative Classification of Membrane Transport Mechanisms:) Tj
T*
(   1. Simple Diffusion:) Tj
T*
(   - Thermodynamics: Passive downhill movement \(Delta G < 0\) along concentration gradient.) Tj
T*
(   - Mechanism: Direct passage through phospholipid bilayer without protein assistance.) Tj
T*
(   - Kinetics: Non-saturable, linear flux \(J = P * Delta C\).) Tj
T*
(   - Examples: O2, CO2, NO, steroid hormones, ethanol.) Tj
T*
(   2. Facilitated Diffusion:) Tj
T*
(   - Thermodynamics: Passive downhill movement \(Delta G < 0\); no metabolic energy consumed.) Tj
T*
(   - Mechanism: Transmembrane protein-mediated \(carriers or channels\).) Tj
T*
(   - Kinetics: Saturable; exhibits Michaelis-Menten kinetics \(Vmax and Km\).) Tj
T*
(   - Examples: GLUT1 glucose uniporter in RBCs; Aquaporins; K+ leak channels.) Tj
T*
(   3. Primary Active Transport:) Tj
T*
(   - Thermodynamics: Uphill movement against electrochemical gradient \(Delta G > 0\).) Tj
T*
(   - Mechanism: Directly coupled to chemical bond cleavage \(ATP hydrolysis\) or light absorption.) Tj
T*
(   - Examples: Na+/K+ ATPase, SERCA Ca2+ pump, Gastric H+/K+ ATPase, ABC transporters.) Tj
T*
(   4. Secondary Active Transport:) Tj
T*
(   - Thermodynamics: Uphill transport of solute coupled to downhill flow of an ion.) Tj
T*
(   - Mechanism: Utilizes pre-existing electrochemical gradient established by a primary pump.) Tj
T*
(   - Examples: SGLT1 \(Na+/glucose symport\), NCX \(3Na+/1Ca2+ antiport\), NHE1 \(Na+/H+ antiporter\).) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(--------------------------------------------------------------------------------) Tj
T*
