(   [Problem Statement]: Detail the molecular mechanism of SGLT-1 and explain how oral rehydration therapy \(ORT\) leverages this transport pathway.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   SGLT-1 Secondary Active Transport and Oral Rehydration Therapy:) Tj
T*
(   1. Molecular Mechanism of SGLT-1:) Tj
T*
(   The Sodium-Glucose Cotransporter 1 \(SGLT1\) is located in the apical brush-border membrane of) Tj
T*
(   enterocytes. It operates with a strict stoichiometry of 2 Na+ : 1 Glucose:) Tj
T*
(   - Step 1: Outward-facing empty carrier binds two extracellular Na+ ions, driven by the steep) Tj
T*
(   extracellular Na+ concentration \(140 mM\).) Tj
T*
(   - Step 2: Na+ binding induces an allosteric conformational shift dramatically increasing) Tj
T*
(   glucose affinity.) Tj
T*
(   - Step 3: D-glucose binds, inducing reorientation to the inward-facing state.) Tj
T*
(   - Step 4: Low cytoplasmic [Na+] \(12 mM\) and negative membrane potential cause Na+ dissociation) Tj
T*
(   into cytoplasm, followed by glucose release.) Tj
T*
(   - Step 5: Empty transporter flips back to face the intestinal lumen.) Tj
T*
(   2. Physiological Basis of Oral Rehydration Therapy \(ORT\):) Tj
T*
(   In cholera, Vibrio cholerae toxin causes massive secretory diarrhea via ADP-ribosylation of) Tj
T*
(   Gs-alpha, continuously activating adenylyl cyclase and opening CFTR chloride channels. However,) Tj
T*
(   SGLT1 remains completely intact and unaffected by cAMP elevation!) Tj
T*
(   Formulating an oral solution with 75 mM NaCl and 75 mM Glucose drives massive coupled Na+ and) Tj
T*
(   glucose absorption via SGLT1. The accumulating intracellular osmolarity pulls water passively) Tj
T*
(   from the lumen into enterocytes via aquaporins, reversing fatal dehydration.) Tj
T*
