(   [Problem Statement]: Discuss the biochemical mechanisms of vesicular docking and fusion mediated by SNARE proteins and Rab GTPases.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   Molecular Mechanism of SNARE and Rab-Mediated Vesicle Fusion:) Tj
T*
(   1. Rab GTPase Targeting and Tethering:) Tj
T*
(   Rab proteins are monomeric GTPases that cycle between inactive GDP-bound \(cytoplasmic, bound to) Tj
T*
(   GDI\) and active GTP-bound states \(membrane-anchored via prenyl tails\). Specific active Rabs) Tj
T*
(   \(e.g., Rab1 for ER-to-Golgi, Rab5 for early endosomes\) recruit long coiled-coil tethering) Tj
T*
(   factors from the target membrane, capturing transport vesicles over distances > 100 nm.) Tj
T*
(   2. SNARE Complex Assembly \(trans-SNARE Formation\):) Tj
T*
(   Fusion requires pairing between:) Tj
T*
(   - v-SNARE \(vesicle SNARE, e.g., Synaptobrevin/VAMP\) on the vesicle membrane \(contributes 1) Tj
T*
(   alpha-helix\).) Tj
T*
(   - t-SNARE \(target SNARE, e.g., Syntaxin-1 contributing 1 alpha-helix, and SNAP-25 contributing) Tj
T*
(   2 alpha-helices\) on the target plasma membrane.) Tj
T*
(   The four alpha-helices assemble into an extraordinarily stable parallel 4-helix coiled-coil) Tj
T*
(   bundle, zippering from the N-terminus toward the membrane-anchored C-terminus.) Tj
T*
(   3. Hemifusion to Full Pore Opening:) Tj
T*
(   The mechanical tension of SNARE zippering pulls the two opposing lipid bilayers within 1.5 nm,) Tj
T*
