(   [Problem Statement]: Explain the biochemical mechanisms by which cells maintain asymmetric phospholipid distribution across the plasma membrane.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   Biochemical Maintenance of Plasma Membrane Phospholipid Asymmetry:) Tj
T*
(   1. Asymmetric Distribution Profile:) Tj
T*
(   - Outer Leaflet: Enriched in choline-containing lipids: Phosphatidylcholine \(PC\) and) Tj
T*
(   Sphingomyelin \(SM\), plus glycolipids.) Tj
T*
(   - Inner Leaflet: Enriched in aminophospholipids: Phosphatidylethanolamine \(PE\) and negatively) Tj
T*
(   charged Phosphatidylserine \(PS\), as well as Phosphatidylinositol \(PI\).) Tj
T*
(   2. Translocase Enzymes:) Tj
T*
(   - Flippases \(P4-type ATPases\): Specifically translocate PS and PE from the outer leaflet to the) Tj
T*
(   inner leaflet against concentration gradients, powered by ATP hydrolysis. Mutations in) Tj
T*
(   flippases disrupt asymmetry.) Tj
T*
(   - Floppases \(ABC Transporters, e.g., ABCB4\): Transport choline phospholipids and cholesterol) Tj
T*
(   from inner to outer leaflet using ATP.) Tj
T*
(   - Scramblases: Calcium-dependent, ATP-independent bidirectional translocators that collapse) Tj
T*
(   lipid asymmetry. Activated during platelet coagulation and apoptosis.) Tj
T*
(   3. Physiological Significance of Asymmetry:) Tj
T*
(   - Apoptosis Detection: In healthy cells, flippases keep PS confined to the inner leaflet.) Tj
T*
(   During apoptosis, flippases are cleaved by caspases while scramblases are activated by Ca2+) Tj
T*
(   influx. PS exposure on the outer leaflet acts as an 'eat-me' signal recognized by macrophage) Tj
T*
(   scavenger receptors.) Tj
T*
(   - Coagulation Cascade: PS exposure on activated platelets provides an essential catalytic) Tj
T*
(   surface for assembling the tenase and prothrombinase enzyme complexes.) Tj
T*
