(   [Problem Statement]: Detail the structure and catalytic cycle of F0F1 ATP Synthase and explain how the proton motive force drives rotary catalysis.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   F0F1 ATP Synthase Structure and Rotary Catalysis \(Boyer's Binding Change Model\):) Tj
T*
(   1. Structural Architecture:) Tj
T*
(   - F0 Subunit \(Membrane-Embedded Rotor\): Consists of a ring of hydrophobic c-subunits \(8-14) Tj
T*
(   copies\) associated with a stator a-subunit having two half-channels for protons.) Tj
T*
(   - F1 Subunit \(Soluble Catalytic Stator\): A hexameric \(alpha-beta\)3 ring projecting into the) Tj
T*
(   mitochondrial matrix, capped by a central gamma-epsilon stalk connected to the c-ring.) Tj
T*
(   2. Proton Motive Force \(pmf\):) Tj
T*
(   pmf = Delta Psi - \(2.303*R*T / F\) * Delta pH.) Tj
T*
(   Protons enter the first half-channel in subunit a, protonate a conserved carboxylate residue) Tj
T*
(   \(Asp-61 / Glu-58\) on a c-subunit, neutralizing its charge and allowing rotation into the lipid) Tj
T*
(   bilayer. After a full revolution, the c-subunit meets the second half-channel, releasing) Tj
T*
(   protons into the matrix.) Tj
T*
