(   [Problem Statement]: Explain the mechanism of primary active transport in the Gastric H+/K+ ATPase and how proton pump inhibitors \(PPIs\) inhibit acid secretion.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   Gastric H+/K+ ATPase Mechanism and PPI Pharmacological Inhibition:) Tj
T*
(   1. Transport Mechanism:) Tj
T*
(   The gastric H+/K+ ATPase is a P-type ATPase localized to tubulovesicles and apical canaliculi) Tj
T*
(   of stomach parietal cells. It pumps H+ into the gastric lumen in exchange for K+ at a 1:1) Tj
T*
(   stoichiometry, powered by ATP hydrolysis:) Tj
T*
(   - Generates the steepest cation concentration gradient in the human body: pumping protons) Tj
T*
(   against a 1,000,000-fold concentration gradient \(intracellular pH 7.2 vs luminal pH 0.8\).) Tj
T*
(   - Operates via the classical E1-E2 cycle, forming a phosphorylated aspartyl intermediate.) Tj
T*
(   2. Mechanism of Action of Proton Pump Inhibitors \(PPIs, e.g., Omeprazole\):) Tj
T*
(   - PPIs are prodrugs administered as weak bases that accumulate selectively in the highly acidic) Tj
T*
(   canalicular space of parietal cells.) Tj
T*
(   - At pH < 1.0, the drug undergoes acid-catalyzed rearrangement into a reactive planar) Tj
T*
(   tetracyclic sulfenamide.) Tj
T*
(   - The cationic sulfenamide forms a covalent, irreversible disulfide bond with accessible) Tj
T*
(   cysteine residues \(predominantly Cys-813\) in the extracellular vestibule of the catalytic) Tj
T*
(   alpha-subunit.) Tj
T*
(   - Covalent adduct formation permanently halts E1-E2 conformational transitions, providing) Tj
T*
(   profound, long-lasting suppression of gastric acid secretion until newly synthesized pump) Tj
T*
(   enzymes are synthesized \(half-life ~24-48 hours\).) Tj
T*
