/F1 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(   [Problem Statement]: Discuss the biochemical composition, assembly, and function of the Nuclear Lamina, and explain laminopathies caused by LMNA mutations.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   Nuclear Lamina Architecture and Laminopathy Pathology:) Tj
T*
(   1. Structure and Composition:) Tj
T*
(   The nuclear lamina is a 10-30 nm dense fibrous meshwork underlying the inner nuclear membrane.) Tj
T*
(   It is assembled from Type V intermediate filament proteins called Lamins:) Tj
T*
(   - B-type Lamins \(Lamin B1, B2\): Permanently farnesylated at their C-terminal CaaX motif,) Tj
T*
(   anchoring them into the inner nuclear membrane via the LBR \(Lamin B Receptor\).) Tj
T*
(   - A-type Lamins \(Lamin A, C\): Encoded by the single LMNA gene via alternative splicing. Lamin A) Tj
T*
(   is synthesized as prelamin A, farnesylated, then cleaved by the metalloprotease ZMPSTE24 to) Tj
T*
(   release mature unfarnesylated lamin A.) Tj
T*
(   2. Cellular Functions:) Tj
T*
ET

